# Fleet choices about the mission itself, and the approach mystery thread.
# Arc and plants: content/STORY_BIBLE.md (spoilers). Voice: content/STYLE.md.

card dec_halfway
type decision
cat mission
days 19 23
weight 14
once
duration 1
roles A B
lean warm feast quiet
text Halfway. Earth and Mars are the same distance behind and ahead, give or take a rounding error. {A} wants to break out the good rations and mark it properly. {B} would rather nobody counted out loud yet.
option feast | Mark it | The fleet eats the good rations on seven screens at once. {A} gives a toast about the halfway mark, and it turns into a toast about the people who packed the food.
  res food -2
  morale A 4
  morale B 2
option quiet | Let it pass | Halfway passes without a word on the fleet channel. On every ship, at the same minute, someone looks at the clock anyway.
  bond A B 3

card dec_name
type decision
cat mission
days 26 34
weight 10
once
duration 2
roles A B
lean curious after home
text The imaging pass has turned up an unnamed crater near the landing zone, small enough that the fleet gets to suggest a name. {A} wants to name it after the first person who ever wrote a Mars mission plan. {B} wants to name it after somewhere at home.
option after | Honor the planners | The name goes in the request. Somewhere, a committee will argue about it for years, which {A} considers part of the honor.
  trait A curious 2
option home | Name it for home | The crater gets the name of a small town on Earth that nobody outside it has heard of. Within a day, the town's newspaper has a front page about it.
  trait B warm 3
  morale B 4

card dec_science
type decision
cat mission
days 14 30
weight 8
once
duration 2
roles A B
lean curious record save
text A gamma-ray burst lights up the sky somewhere past the galaxy, and the fleet is the farthest instrument humanity has. Holding attitude for the measurement costs propellant. {A} wants the data. {B} wants the propellant.
option record | Hold for the data | Seven ships hold still for twenty minutes, and a burst that left its source before Earth had oceans is triangulated from a baseline nobody has had before. {A} reads the confirmation and lets out a breath nobody else hears.
  res fuel -2
  trait A curious 3
  morale A 3
option save | Keep the propellant | The fleet passes. Ground telescopes get the burst anyway, from one angle. {B} logs the propellant saved, and doesn't pretend it was an easy trade.
  trait B brave 2

# --- Night-side flashes (part 1-4) -------------------------------------------------
card m_flash1
type event
cat mystery
clue core
due
once
days 29 32
roles A
when cond A ok
text {A} pulls the orbiters' night frames of the landing zone, the way people look at a house before moving in. On dark ground near the edge, one pixel is lit. On the next frame it isn't. A cosmic-ray hit on the sensor, almost certainly. {A} writes down both timestamps anyway.
do set flash_seen
do mark t_flash
do who flash_who A
do trait A curious 2

card m_flash2
type event
cat mystery
days 31 35
weight 30
once
roles A
when flag flash_seen
when since t_flash >= 2
when cond A ok
text {A} goes through every night frame of that patch from this month. They're minutes apart, too coarse to show a rhythm, but whenever a pixel there is lit, it's the same pixel. Cosmic rays don't come back to the same address. {A} sends the list to the fleet, and the channel goes quiet.
do set flash_twice

card dec_flash
type decision
cat mystery
days 32 38
weight 40
once
duration 1
roles A B
when flag flash_twice
lean curious look leave
text The lit pixel is in the newest frames too. {A} wants to ask the ground to task an orbiter for a fast sequence over it, at the cost of a day of the fleet's downlink: every letter from home waits. {B} would leave it for the people who'll be down there with time to look.
option look | Ask for the sequence | The request goes up. {A} writes the apology to six inboxes personally, and means each one.
  set flash_looked
  morale A -2
  morale B -2
option leave | Leave it for the ground | The pixel goes into the anomaly log, under a heading {B} writes and then underlines.
  set flash_left

card flash_answer
type event
cat mystery
clue
due
once
days 34 39
roles A
when flag flash_looked
when since t_flash >= 4
when cond A ok
text The fast sequence comes down overnight: forty frames of dark ground. The pixel lights, goes out, lights again, and never moves against the rocks around it. Whatever it is, it isn't in orbit. {A} counts the frames between flashes, gets the same number every time, and calls the other six ships.
do trait A curious 4
do morale A 2

card m_overlay
type event
cat mystery
clue
due
once
days 37 39
roles A
when fired flash_answer
when cond A ok
text {A} lays the old dish log over the fast sequence, frame by frame, because nobody else has had time. The carrier peaks on the exact frame the pixel lights. Every time. {A} prints both traces and tapes them up side by side, and doesn't take them down.
do trait A curious 3

# --- Middle-dark plant (a careful reader's detail; ordinary explanations exist) ---
card m_ripple
type event
cat mystery
clue
days 13 24
weight 14
once
roles A
when cond A ok
text {A} cleans up the dish noise floor for the weekly report and finds a faint ripple in it, period about eleven seconds. Reaction wheel harmonic, most likely. {A} notes it in the margin and moves on to the propellant tables.
do trait A curious 1

# --- The crew's theory (misdirection; see STORY_BIBLE.md) -------------------------
card m_theory
type event
cat mystery
due
once
days 17 24
roles A
when fired dec_signal
when since t_signal >= 4
when cond A ok
text {A} finds a match in the public catalog of retired spacecraft: a relay orbiter that went quiet over Mars years ago, last tracked spinning at five and a half turns a minute. Eleven seconds a turn, near enough. {A} draws its orbit on the galley whiteboard and sleeps straight through for once.
do morale A 3

# --- The payoff: fires on day 40 in every season ------------------------------------
card m_reveal
type event
cat mystery
reveal
due
once
days 40 40
roles A
when is A flash_who
when cond A ok
text The orbiters' landing-zone survey comes in sharp. Where the old thumbnail showed a smudge and a streak, a ship stands on its legs, tilted a few degrees: Cargo 2. In the night video its lights blink, then blink again eleven seconds later. {A} looks back at the thumbnail. The streak was its shadow.
do trait A curious 3

card m_reveal_any
type event
cat mystery
reveal
due
once
days 40 40
roles A
when !fired m_reveal
text The orbiters' landing-zone survey comes in sharp. Where the old thumbnail showed a smudge and a streak, a ship stands on its legs, tilted a few degrees: Cargo 2. In the night video its lights blink, then blink again eleven seconds later. {A} looks back at the thumbnail. The streak was its shadow.
do trait A curious 3

# --- Day 41: the descent brief, one per arrival branch ----------------------------
card a_descent_hold
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when flag arrive_hold
when cond A ok
text Capture burn, on time. The storm fills the landing zone from rim to rim, and tomorrow's entry plan has a line in it nobody has had to write before: "if radar degrades, aim for the light". {A} reads it out loud to the empty cabin, to hear how it sounds.
do morale A 2

card a_descent_wait
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when flag arrive_wait
when cond A ok
text Capture into the long orbit. Below, the storm lies over the landing zone like a hand over a mouth. On the night pass {A} finds the light through the dust, faint and on time, and then sits down with the ration tables to work out how many nights of waiting the fleet can afford.
do morale A -2

card a_descent_clear
type event
cat arrival
due
once
days 41 41
roles A
when !flag arrive_hold
when !flag arrive_wait
when cond A ok
text Capture burn, on time, into a clear sky. The landing zone is exactly where the maps said, with one more thing on it than the maps said. {A} packs a bag for a walk: water, a torch, a roll of tape, a marker. Old habits.
do morale A 3

